Found it. `groundHeightAt` line 243: `if (x >= g.min.x && x <= g.max2.x && ...)`. So `g.min` or `g.max2` is undefined for some ground zone. That means some ground zone object doesn't have `min`/`max2` set properly.

Looking at addGroundZone:
```
function addGroundZone(x0, z0, x1, z1, height) {
  const g = { min: new THREE.Vector2(x0, z0), max: new THREE.Vector2(x1, z1), height };
  groundZones.push(g);
  return g;
}
```
I renamed `max` to `max2` in groundHeightAt but addGroundZone sets `max`, not `max2`! So `g.max2` is undefined → `g.max2.x` throws.

That's the bug. Let me fix: either rename in addGroundZone to max2, or in groundHeightAt use g.max. Consistency: use `max`. Let me update groundHeightAt to read `g.max`.

